// Bit standard library — tz module: the IANA time zone database (#5098).
//
// `data` (./data.bit, generated by tools/gentzdata from a host's
// /usr/share/zoneinfo, #5097) is the blob `std/time`'s `extend()` consumes:
//
//   import * as time from "std/time"
//   import * as tz from "std/tz"
//   time.extend(tz.data)
//
// `std/time` never imports this module — the dependency runs one way, so a
// program that imports only `std/time` never pays for the database. Only a
// program that also imports `std/tz` links `data`'s bytes in.
//
// Host zone files always win: `zone()` (stdlib/time/zone.bit) reads the
// running system's own zoneinfo tree first and only falls back to whatever
// `extend()` published when that lookup fails. This database exists for
// hosts with no zoneinfo tree at all — containers, for instance — not to
// override one that is already present.
//
// `release` (./data.bit) is the IANA release id the checked-in `data` was
// generated from, exactly as the source `+VERSION` file held it. Regenerate
// with `bit run tools/gentzdata -- [zoneinfo-dir] [out-file]` (defaults:
// /usr/share/zoneinfo, stdlib/tz/data.bit) when a newer release is needed —
// governments change DST rules often enough that this is a routine, one
// command operation, and `release` gives "which tzdata are we on" an
// auditable answer in the tree. Do not hand-edit ./data.bit.
